<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Migration</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1910">1910</a>–<a href="/years/1970">1970</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Great Northward Migration, Black Migration</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Migration was the movement of six million Black Americans out of the rural Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to the urban Northeast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midwestern-united-states">Midwest</a> and West between 1910 and 1970. It was driven by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/racial-segregation">racial segregation</a> and discrimination under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jim-crow-laws">Jim Crow laws</a> and by lynchings, and drawn by labor shortages in Northern factories during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>. Historians divide it into a first migration (1910–1940) of about 1.6 million people and a second (1940–1970) of at least five million. Most migrants settled in the largest cities, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City, Chicago, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/detroit">Detroit</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a>, where they built culturally influential communities; by 1970 more than 80% of Black Americans lived in cities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-migration-african-american.jpg" alt="Great Migration" width="330" height="258" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: undetermined; published in The Chicago Defender on September 4, 1920 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_migration.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Great Migration</b> took millions of Black southerners to the cities of the North and West, where they could vote but met segregated housing and closed trades.</blockquote><a href="/movement/civil-rights-movement">Civil rights movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early 20th century the <b>Great Migration</b> carried African Americans out of the Jim Crow South to the cities of the Northeast and Midwest, in search of a better life and relief from institutionalised racism, while people of African descent came from the racially stratified communities of…</blockquote><a href="/movement/harlem-renaissance">Harlem Renaissance · Background</a></li></ul></section>
